<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="reed-quadratic-solving">
  <title>Solving with the quadratic formula</title>
  <p>The quadratic formula gives a systematic way to find the solutions
  (roots) of any quadratic equation.</p>
  <assemblage>
    <title>The quadratic formula</title>
    <p>For real coefficients <m>a,b,c</m> with <m>a\ne0</m>, the
    solutions of <m>ax^2+bx+c=0</m> are</p>
    <p><md>\boxed{x=\frac{-b\pm\sqrt{b^2-4ac}}{2a}.}</md></p>
    <p>The symbol <m>\pm</m> asks you to calculate two expressions:
    one with a plus and one with a minus. If the square root is zero, the
    two expressions give the same solution. If <m>b^2-4ac&lt;0</m>, there
    are no real solutions; complex solutions are discussed further below.</p>
  </assemblage>
  <paragraphs>
    <title>A reliable procedure</title>
    <p><ol>
      <li><p>Move all terms to one side, combine like terms, and write
      <m>ax^2+bx+c=0</m>.</p></li>
      <li><p>Identify <m>a,b,c</m>, including their signs. Use zero for
      any missing linear or constant term.</p></li>
      <li><p>Compute the <term>discriminant</term>
      <m>D=b^2-4ac</m>.</p></li>
      <li><p>Substitute into the formula with parentheses around negative
      coefficients. Simplify the square root, then calculate both the
      <m>+</m> and <m>-</m> solutions. The denominator <m>2a</m> divides
      the entire numerator.</p></li>
      <li><p>Check by substituting your answers into the original equation.
      Keep exact roots unless a decimal approximation is requested.</p></li>
    </ol></p>
  </paragraphs>
  <table xml:id="reed-quadratic-discriminant">
    <title>Let the discriminant predict the result</title>
    <tabular>
      <row header="yes"><cell>Discriminant</cell><cell>Real solutions</cell><cell>Graph of <m>y=ax^2+bx+c</m></cell></row>
      <row><cell><m>D&gt;0</m></cell><cell>Two distinct real roots</cell><cell>Crosses the x-axis twice</cell></row>
      <row><cell><m>D=0</m></cell><cell>One distinct real root, <m>-b/(2a)</m></cell><cell>Touches the x-axis at its vertex</cell></row>
      <row><cell><m>D&lt;0</m></cell><cell>No real roots</cell><cell>Does not meet the x-axis</cell></row>
    </tabular>
  </table>
  <example xml:id="reed-quadratic-example-rational">
    <statement><p>Solve <m>2x^2+x=3</m>.</p></statement>
    <solution>
      <p><ol>
        <li><p>Subtract <m>3</m> from both sides to obtain
        <m>2x^2+x-3=0</m>.</p></li>
        <li><p>Thus <m>a=2</m>, <m>b=1</m>, and <m>c=-3</m>.
        Note that <m>c</m> is not <m>3</m>!</p></li>
        <li><p>The discriminant is <m>D=1^2-4(2)(-3)=25</m>, so the
        equation will have two real solutions.</p></li>
        <li><p><md>x=\frac{-1\pm\sqrt{25}}{2(2)}
        =\frac{-1\pm5}{4}.</md></p>
        <p>The plus branch gives <m>x=1</m>; the minus branch gives
        <m>x=-3/2</m>.</p></li>
        <li><p>Check in the original equation: <m>2(1)^2+1=3</m> and
        <m>2(-3/2)^2+(-3/2)=9/2-3/2=3</m>.</p></li>
      </ol></p>
    </solution>
  </example>
  <exercise xml:id="reed-quadratic-live-formula">
    <webwork>
      <pg-code><![CDATA[
Context("Numeric");
$a = random(1,4,1);
$b = non_zero_random(-7,7,1);
# A negative constant gives two real roots. Avoid a square discriminant so
# this practice calls for the quadratic formula rather than integer factoring.
do {
    $c = random(-6,-1,1);
    $D = $b*$b - 4*$a*$c;
} while (int(sqrt($D))**2 == $D);
$f = Formula("$a*x^2+($b)*x+($c)")->reduce;
$disc = Real($D);
$xplus = Compute("(-($b)+sqrt($D))/(2*$a)");
$xminus = Compute("(-($b)-sqrt($D))/(2*$a)");
      ]]></pg-code>
      <statement>
        <p>Use the quadratic formula to solve</p>
        <p><md><var name="$f"/>=0.</md></p>
        <p>Identify <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, and <m>c</m> on paper, then
        enter the discriminant and both solutions. Enter exact expressions;
        for example, type <c>sqrt(5)</c> for <m>\sqrt{5}</m>.</p>
        <p>Discriminant: <m>D=b^2-4ac=</m>
        <var name="$disc" width="8"/>.</p>
        <p>Solution using the plus sign: <m>x_+=</m>
        <var name="$xplus" width="25"/>.</p>
        <p>Solution using the minus sign: <m>x_-=</m>
        <var name="$xminus" width="25"/>.</p>
        <p>Use <q>Check Responses</q> to check each answer and
        <q>Randomize</q> to try a new equation.</p>
      </statement>
      <hint><p>Keep the signs of <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, and <m>c</m>.
      Square all of <m>b</m>, including its sign. Divide the entire
      numerator by <m>2a</m>, and calculate both branches of
      <m>x=(-b\pm\sqrt{D})/(2a)</m>.</p></hint>
      <solution>
        <p>Here <m>a=<var name="$a"/></m>,
        <m>b=<var name="$b"/></m>, and <m>c=<var name="$c"/></m>.
        Therefore</p>
        <p><md>D=(<var name="$b"/>)^2
        -4(<var name="$a"/>)(<var name="$c"/>)=<var name="$D"/>.</md></p>
        <p>Substitute into the two branches:</p>
        <p><md>x_+=\frac{-(<var name="$b"/>)+\sqrt{<var name="$D"/>}}{2(<var name="$a"/>)},
        \qquad
        x_-=\frac{-(<var name="$b"/>)-\sqrt{<var name="$D"/>}}{2(<var name="$a"/>)}.</md></p>
        <p>Equivalent exact expressions are accepted. The positive
        discriminant gives two distinct real solutions.</p>
      </solution>
    </webwork>
  </exercise>
  <paragraphs xml:id="reed-quadratic-factoring">
    <title>Factoring</title>
    <p><term>Factoring</term> means rewriting an expression as a product
    of simpler expressions, called <term>factors</term>. For example,
    <m>x^2+5x+6=(x+2)(x+3)</m>: the factors are <m>x+2</m> and
    <m>x+3</m>. Factoring reverses expanding, which uses the distributive
    property to multiply out the factors.</p>
    <p>For a quadratic beginning with <m>x^2</m>, one useful method is
    to look for two numbers whose sum is the coefficient of <m>x</m> and
    whose product is the constant term. Here <m>2+3=5</m> and
    <m>2\cdot3=6</m>, so we try <m>(x+2)(x+3)</m>. Expanding checks
    the factorization:</p>
    <p><md>(x+2)(x+3)=x^2+3x+2x+6=x^2+5x+6.</md></p>
    <p>Note that the equation from the previous example factors as
    <m>(2x+3)(x-1)=0</m>. We can check this by expanding and simplifying:</p>
    <p><md>(2x+3)(x-1)=2x^2-2x+3x-3=2x^2+x-3.</md></p>
    <p>When considering the solutions to the equation
    <m>(2x+3)(x-1)=0</m>, we know that at least one factor must be zero,
    since the only way two numbers multiply to be zero is if at least one
    of them is zero. Thus <m>2x+3=0</m> or <m>x-1=0</m>, again giving
    the same solutions we found with the quadratic formula:
    <m>-3/2</m> and <m>1</m>.</p>
    <p>Both methods find the same zeros. More generally, if a quadratic has
    real roots <m>r_1,r_2</m> (including a possible repeated root), then</p>
    <p><md>ax^2+bx+c=a(x-r_1)(x-r_2).</md></p>
    <p>The leading coefficient <m>a</m> must be included. For example,
    <m>2(x+3/2)(x-1)=(2x+3)(x-1)</m>. A root <m>r</m> corresponds
    to a factor <m>x-r</m>: a root of <m>-3/2</m> gives
    <m>x+3/2</m>, not <m>x-3/2</m>.</p>
    <p>Factoring can be a quick way to avoid having to go through the full
    process of using the quadratic formula. However, if you do not see
    factors quickly, the formula will always work.</p>
  </paragraphs>
  <exercise xml:id="reed-quadratic-live-factoring">
    <introduction><p>This instant-feedback problem is reused from
    <xref ref="factoring" text="title"/> in Part I. Factor the expression
    in the answer box. After checking your factorization, set each factor
    equal to zero on paper to find the roots; confirm them with the formula.</p></introduction>
    <!-- Reused from Part I: Factoring, exercise 12; also in Math 111. -->
    <webwork source="Contrib/CCCS/CollegeAlgebra/Prerequisite_Review/CCD_CCCS_Openstax_AlgTrig_AT-1-001-AS_1_5_F.pg"/>
  </exercise>
  <example xml:id="reed-quadratic-example-irrational">
    <statement><p>Solve <m>x^2-4x+1=0</m> exactly.</p></statement>
    <solution>
      <p>Here <m>a=1</m>, <m>b=-4</m>, <m>c=1</m>, so
      <m>D=(-4)^2-4(1)(1)=12</m>. Notice that
      <m>(-4)^2=16</m>. Therefore</p>
      <p><md>x=\frac{-(-4)\pm\sqrt{12}}{2}
      =\frac{4\pm2\sqrt{3}}{2}=2\pm\sqrt{3}.</md></p>
      <p>These are two exact real roots, approximately <m>0.268</m> and
      <m>3.732</m>. The real factorization is
      <m>(x-(2+\sqrt{3}))(x-(2-\sqrt{3}))</m>. To check both at once,
      rewrite the original expression as <m>(x-2)^2-3</m>.
      Substituting either root gives <m>(\pm\sqrt{3})^2-3=0</m>.</p>
    </solution>
  </example>
  <p>The following two additional examples compare the cases
  <m>D=0</m> and <m>D&lt;0</m>.</p>
  <example xml:id="reed-quadratic-example-repeated">
    <statement><p>Solve <m>x^2-6x+9=0</m> and describe what happens
    on the graph.</p></statement>
    <solution><p>Here <m>(a,b,c)=(1,-6,9)</m>, so
    <m>D=(-6)^2-4(1)(9)=0</m>. The formula gives</p>
    <p><md>x=\frac{6\pm\sqrt{0}}{2}=3.</md></p>
    <p>Both branches coincide. Factoring gives <m>(x-3)^2=0</m>, so
    <m>3</m> is a repeated root. The graph touches the x-axis at
    its vertex <m>(3,0)</m>.</p></solution>
  </example>
  <example xml:id="reed-quadratic-example-complex">
    <statement><p>Solve <m>x^2+2x+5=0</m> over the real numbers.
    If complex numbers are available, find those solutions too.</p></statement>
    <solution><p>Here <m>(a,b,c)=(1,2,5)</m> and
    <m>D=2^2-4(1)(5)=-16</m>. There are no real solutions, since
    no real square root of <m>-16</m> exists. The graph has no x-intercept.</p>
    <p>Over the complex numbers, use <m>i^2=-1</m> and
    <m>\sqrt{-16}=4i</m>:</p>
    <p><md>x=\frac{-2\pm4i}{2}=-1\pm2i.</md></p>
    <p>This quadratic cannot be factored into real linear factors.</p></solution>
  </example>
  <assemblage>
    <title>Watch the signs and the fraction</title>
    <p>If <m>b=-4</m>, then <m>-b=4</m> and <m>b^2=16</m>.
    Write <m>(-4)^2</m> on a calculator. Also,
    <m>(4\pm2\sqrt{3})/2=2\pm\sqrt{3}</m> because both terms
    are divided by <m>2</m>. You cannot cancel a factor from just one
    term in a sum. Finally, the zero-product property applies only when
    the product equals zero.</p>
  </assemblage>
  <paragraphs>
    <title>Practice: try first, then reveal the solution</title>
    <p>Work on paper. For each equation, record standard form, the
    coefficients, and the discriminant before finding the roots. Each
    exercise has an expandable solution for checking your reasoning.</p>
  </paragraphs>
  <exercise xml:id="reed-quadratic-practice-standard">
    <statement><p>Solve <m>3x^2+5=8x</m> using the formula.
    Then check by factoring.</p></statement>
    <hint><p>Subtract <m>8x</m> first. What is the sign of <m>b</m>?</p></hint>
    <solution><p>Standard form is <m>3x^2-8x+5=0</m>, so
    <m>(a,b,c)=(3,-8,5)</m> and <m>D=64-60=4</m>.
    The formula gives <m>x=(8\pm2)/6</m>, hence
    <m>x=5/3</m> or <m>x=1</m>. Factoring gives
    <m>(3x-5)(x-1)=0</m> with the same solutions.</p></solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise xml:id="reed-quadratic-practice-irrational">
    <statement><p>Solve <m>2x^2+4x-1=0</m> exactly. Can this
    polynomial be factored into linear factors with rational coefficients?</p></statement>
    <hint><p>Simplify <m>\sqrt{24}</m> before reducing the fraction.</p></hint>
    <solution><p><m>(a,b,c)=(2,4,-1)</m> and <m>D=16+8=24</m>.
    Thus <m>x=(-4\pm\sqrt{24})/4=-1\pm\sqrt{6}/2</m>.
    Both roots are irrational, so no factorization into rational linear
    factors exists.</p></solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise xml:id="reed-quadratic-practice-repeated">
    <statement><p>Solve <m>4x^2-12x+9=0</m>. How many distinct
    real zeros does the corresponding function have? Describe its graph
    at the x-axis.</p></statement>
    <solution><p><m>(a,b,c)=(4,-12,9)</m> and <m>D=144-144=0</m>.
    Thus <m>x=12/8=3/2</m>. There is one distinct real zero, repeated
    twice in <m>(2x-3)^2</m>. The upward-opening parabola touches the
    x-axis at its vertex <m>(3/2,0)</m>.</p></solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise xml:id="reed-quadratic-practice-no-real">
    <statement><p>Determine the real solutions of <m>x^2+2x+5=0</m>
    using its discriminant. If you know complex numbers, also find its
    complex solutions.</p></statement>
    <solution><p><m>(a,b,c)=(1,2,5)</m> gives <m>D=4-20=-16</m>.
    There are no real solutions. Over the complex numbers,
    <m>x=(-2\pm4i)/2=-1\pm2i</m>.</p></solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise xml:id="reed-quadratic-practice-missing">
    <statement><p>Solve <m>3x^2=12</m> using the formula and then
    directly using square roots. Explain why <m>b=0</m> does not prevent
    use of the formula.</p></statement>
    <solution><p>Write <m>3x^2+0x-12=0</m>. Then
    <m>(a,b,c)=(3,0,-12)</m>, <m>D=144</m>, and
    <m>x=(0\pm12)/6=\pm2</m>. Directly, <m>x^2=4</m> gives
    <m>x=\pm2</m>. Only <m>a</m> must be nonzero; a zero value for
    <m>b</m> is allowed.</p></solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise xml:id="reed-quadratic-practice-error">
    <statement><p>For <m>2x^2-3x-2=0</m>, a student writes
    <m>x=(3\pm5)/2</m>, giving <m>4</m> and <m>-1</m>.
    What went wrong? Find and check the correct solutions.</p></statement>
    <solution><p>The denominator should be <m>2a=4</m>, not
    <m>2</m>. With <m>(a,b,c)=(2,-3,-2)</m> and <m>D=25</m>,
    the roots are <m>(3\pm5)/4</m>, namely <m>2</m> and <m>-1/2</m>.
    Substitution gives <m>8-6-2=0</m> and
    <m>1/2+3/2-2=0</m>.</p></solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise xml:id="reed-quadratic-practice-context">
    <statement><p>A rectangle is <m>3</m> meters longer than it is wide
    and has area <m>18</m> square meters. Let <m>w</m> be its width.
    Write a quadratic equation, use the formula to solve it, and explain
    which solution gives the rectangle's dimensions.</p></statement>
    <solution><p>The area equation is <m>w(w+3)=18</m>, or
    <m>w^2+3w-18=0</m>. Here <m>(a,b,c)=(1,3,-18)</m> and
    <m>D=9+72=81</m>. Thus <m>w=(-3\pm9)/2</m> is <m>3</m> or
    <m>-6</m>. Width must be positive, so the rectangle is <m>3</m>
    meters by <m>6</m> meters. The negative root solves the algebraic
    equation but does not describe a physical width.</p></solution>
  </exercise>
  <p>Previous: <xref ref="reed-quadratic-functions" text="title"/>.
  Next: <xref ref="reed-quadratic-derivation" text="title"/>.</p>
</section>
